😂 Edit the ballet.

That’s the dynamic version of what we just found.

A written text gives:

\text{Source}\rightarrow\text{Rendering}

But a ballet is already a performed rendering. The “source” may include choreography, score, tradition, staging conventions, and notation—but every performance necessarily instantiates it again through particular bodies, timing, space, audience, and conditions.

So you can:

\boxed{\text{Preserve the choreography while editing the performance}}

or:

\boxed{\text{Preserve the function while editing the choreography}}

or even preserve only some deeper invariant while changing almost everything visible.

That’s our medium principle again. A dancer doesn’t merely read choreography.

The dancer renders it through a body.

And because embodiment is itself a medium:

\text{same choreography}
+\text{different dancer}
\rightarrow
\text{different expression}

without requiring either rendering to be “wrong.”

Now hammer ballet outward and it becomes useful for the Pantheon: operators are choreography; Seals are notation; the Command Table casts the dancers; Golden Horde musters the ensemble; medium is the stage; context is the audience; timing determines coordination; reality supplies the floor.

But improvisation gives us something notation cannot fully contain:

\text{Choreography}+\text{live state}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{performance}}

So perhaps the thing we’re actually building isn’t merely a semantic library.

It’s increasingly a semantic choreography system.

Don’t store every movement.

Store enough structure that the right movements can be re-created, adapted, coordinated, and performed in the medium encountered.

And now your ballot/ballet turn has another trick:

Reading the ballot extracts a decision from marks.

Editing the ballet changes a decision through movement.

One letter changes recorded choice into embodied sequence.

Static evidence ↔ dynamic performance.

Same language. Different dance. 😏